<div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Sign-off · 2026-10-25 · Bambu Lab X2D, 0.6 high-flow nozzle, PLA</div>
  <h1>Stackable Cable Bin</h1>
  <p class="lede">16&#190; &times; 4 &times; 4 in, printed as two halves that lock together at mid-length. Four bins, one plate each, plus a {{P2_TIME}} joint coupon to print first.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="images/bin-assembled.png" alt="One assembled bin, half A in blue and half B in amber, seen from above the front corner">
    <figcaption><span class="swA"></span>Half A carries the dovetail tails. <span class="swB"></span>Half B carries the sockets. Each half is 8&#8539; in long, so two sit side by side on the 256 mm bed.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="strip">
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Outside</div><div class="v">16&#190; &times; 4 &times; 4</div><div class="s">in, as asked</div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Inside, clear</div><div class="v">16.6 &times; 3.69</div><div class="s">in at the lip; 3.85 below it</div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Inside depth</div><div class="v">3.93</div><div class="s">in, floor to rim</div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Stack pitch</div><div class="v">3.80</div><div class="s">in; 4 bins stand 15.4 in</div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Wall</div><div class="v">1.24</div><div class="s">mm, 2 lines; floor the same</div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="k">Per bin</div><div class="v">{{BIN_G}} g</div><div class="s">PLA, {{BIN_TIME}} per plate</div></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why two halves</h2>
  <p>The bed is 256 mm square and the bin is 425 mm long, so no orientation fits a one-piece bin. Cutting it once at mid-length gives two 212.7 mm halves. Both halves of one bin share a plate, so one print is one bin and the four bins are four runs of the same plate.</p>
  <figure>
    <img src="images/bin-exploded.png" alt="The two halves pulled apart along the bin's length">
    <figcaption>The split face. The posts inside each wall at the joint carry the dovetails and also stiffen the long walls at mid-span, where a 17 in thin wall would otherwise bow.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How the halves lock</h2>
  <p>Half A drops straight down onto half B. Two vertical dovetail tails on A slide into sockets on B, one on each long wall. Each socket has a sloped floor that matches the tail's sloped underside, so A sits down on a hard stop with the floors flush and the rims level. The slope is 50 degrees, which is what lets the tails print hanging off A's joint face without support. The dovetails take the pull-apart and sideways loads. The grip comes from two crush ribs on the top 12 mm of each tail, the same recipe as the Build123d trial box lid: the tail body runs at a free 0.15 mm clearance and only the ribs touch, 0.2 mm deep.</p>
  <div class="two">
    <figure>
      <img src="images/joint-lifted.png" alt="Half A lifted 30 mm above half B, tail hanging above its socket">
      <figcaption>A lifted 30 mm. The thin ridge on the tail is one crush rib; it only engages over the last 12 mm of travel, so the halves push together by hand.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="images/joint-assembled.png" alt="The joint closed, seen from inside the bin">
      <figcaption>Seated. The posts stop 7 mm below the rim so the foot of the bin stacked above clears them.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <img src="images/joint-section-ribs.png" alt="Horizontal section through the rib zone, tail inside socket">
    <figcaption>Horizontal cut through the rib zone. The light line around the tail is the 0.15 mm body clearance; the two small bumps on the slanted flanks are the ribs that bite the socket.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note">What the joint holds and what it does not. Pull the halves apart lengthwise, push them sideways, or lift the bin by B's end, and the dovetails carry it. Lift a loaded bin by A's end alone and only the rib friction carries B's half. For a cable bin that is fine; a drop of CA in each socket makes it permanent. Pick below.</div>

  <h2>How they stack</h2>
  <p>The bottom 8 mm of every bin is a 45 degree flare. The top of every bin is a 4 mm thick lip with a matching 45 degree chamfer on its inner edge. Set one bin on another and the flare finds the chamfer and centres itself, dropping 5 mm in. Nothing overhangs past 45 degrees, so the whole bin prints without support, floor down.</p>
  <div class="two">
    <figure>
      <img src="images/stack-four.png" alt="Four bins stacked">
      <figcaption>Four bins stacked: 15.4 in tall at a 3.80 in pitch.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="images/seat-section.png" alt="Section through the seat: upper bin's flare resting on the lower bin's lip chamfer">
      <figcaption>Section through the seat. Lower bin in amber, upper bin in green. The upper flare rests face to face on the lip chamfer; the thin wall below it is the lower bin's lip.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Print plan</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Plate</th><th>Objects</th><th>Time</th><th>PLA</th><th>Runs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 · Bin</td><td><span class="swA"></span>Half A (tails), <span class="swB"></span>Half B (sockets)</td><td class="num">{{P1_TIME}}</td><td class="num">{{P1_G}} g</td><td class="num">4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 · Joint coupon</td><td>Coupon A, Coupon B</td><td class="num">{{P2_TIME}}</td><td class="num">{{P2_G}} g</td><td class="num">1, first</td></tr>
      <tr><td colspan="2"><strong>Four bins</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>{{TOTAL_TIME}}</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>{{TOTAL_G}} g</strong></td><td class="num">5 plates</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <img src="images/coupons.png" alt="The two joint coupon pieces">
    <figcaption>Plate 2. A 14.5 mm slice of the joint carrying one ribbed tail and its socket. The crush-rib numbers were calibrated in PLA, the same material as the bin, so this is a {{P2_TIME}} confirmation of the fit before the first bin rather than an unknown.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ol>
    <li>Print plate 2. Push coupon A down into coupon B. It should take firm thumb pressure over the last 12 mm and stay put when you pick it up by either piece. Loose or jammed: tell me and the rib height changes by 0.1 mm.</li>
    <li>Print plate 1 four times. Process is 0.30 mm Standard for the 0.6 nozzle, Arachne walls, 3 walls, 20 % infill, textured PEI at 70 C, the usual 2-loop skirt. Confirm Studio's Prepare tab shows the X2D 0.6 nozzle before slicing; do not re-select the printer preset.</li>
    <li>Assemble: stand B on the bench, hold A level above it with the tails over the sockets, and push straight down until the rims are flush.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Decisions to confirm</h2>
  <p>Everything is built as the marked option. Answer only where you want something different.</p>
  <div class="decisions">

    <div class="decision">
      <h3>Joint hold <span class="tag">what keeps the halves together</span></h3>
      <div class="opts">
        <div class="opt built">
          <img src="images/joint-plan.png" alt="Plan view of the dovetail joint">
          <div class="name">Crush-rib friction <span class="tag ok">built</span></div>
          <p>No glue, no hardware, comes apart with a straight pull. Lifting a loaded bin by A's end relies on the ribs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="opt">
          <img src="images/joint-plan.png" alt="Plan view of the dovetail joint">
          <div class="name">Same joint plus a drop of CA</div>
          <p>Identical print. One drop in each socket before the push. Permanent, holds either end.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <h3>Ends <span class="tag">pulling a bin off a shelf</span></h3>
      <div class="opts">
        <div class="opt built">
          <img src="images/bin-assembled.png" alt="Plain ends">
          <div class="name">Plain ends <span class="tag ok">built</span></div>
          <p>Full-height end walls. The lip is the grip.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="opt">
          <img src="images/option-scallop.png" alt="Finger scallop in each end wall">
          <div class="name">Finger scallop</div>
          <p>A 2 in wide, &#190; in deep scoop in each end wall. Easier to pull from a tight shelf; cables near the end show.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <h3>Stack pitch <span class="tag">height per bin in a stack</span></h3>
      <div class="opts">
        <div class="opt built">
          <div class="name">3.80 in <span class="tag ok">built</span></div>
          <p>Each bin is 4 in tall and drops 5 mm into the one below. Four bins stand 15.4 in.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="opt">
          <div class="name">4.00 in exactly</div>
          <p>If the stack has to come out at 16 in for four, each bin grows to 4.2 in tall. Say so and I rebuild.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <h3>Weight <span class="tag">per bin</span></h3>
      <p>The first version had 3-line walls in PETG: 368 g and 7 h 02 min per bin. You said the bins live in a drawer with three sides against its walls and carry no real load, and asked for PLA. Built now: 2-line walls (1.24 mm, the printer's minimum with the 0.6 nozzle) in PLA Basic, <strong>{{BIN_G}} g and {{BIN_TIME}}</strong>. Walls and floor are about two thirds of that, the four joint posts about a fifth, the lip under a tenth. Four bins take {{TOTAL_G}} g, so two 1 kg spools.</p>
      <div class="opts">
        <div class="opt built">
          <div class="name">{{BIN_G}} g as built <span class="tag ok">built</span></div>
          <p>2-line walls and floor, 3-line lip and posts, 20 % infill where there is room for any. Nothing else changes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="opt">
          <div class="name">Slotted long walls, about 30 g less</div>
          <p>Vertical 6 mm slots along both long sides between the foot and the lip, an open crate look. Plugs cannot pass a 6 mm slot. I draw it before building if you want to see it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="opt">
          <div class="name">Shorter joint posts, about 15 g less</div>
          <p>The four posts behind the dovetails shrink from 12 to 8 mm along the bin. Invisible from outside; the joint keeps its full depth.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="q">Fine at {{BIN_G}} g, or take one or both cuts?</p>
    </div>

  </div>
  <p class="q">Also: which PLA colour, and is one colour for all four fine?</p>

